{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III (c. 1140–1187) was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna and his uncle Baldwin III of Jerusalem acted as regents until he came of age.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Antioch. In 1165 he married Eschiva of Bures who was the heiress of the Principality of Galilee. This marriage strengthened his position in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III was also involved in the succession crisis following the death of King Amalric of Jerusalem in 1174. He supported Miles of Plancy as regent but later backed Raymond of Antioch who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III is best known for his role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He commanded the vanguard of the Crusader army against Saladin. The battle ended in a decisive Muslim victory and Raymond III was one of the few Christian leaders to escape the battlefield. He fled to Tyre where he died later that year. His death marked the end of his line as he had no legitimate heirs.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III was a prominent figure in the history of the Crusader states. His life and career were marked by political maneuvering and military engagements. He was a key player in the complex web of alliances and rivalries that characterized the politics of the region. His death at the Battle of Hattin was a significant blow to the Crusader states and a turning point in their struggle against Saladin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"